# Model Lenses

"Model lenses" is this project's working name for the artifacts that live under
`doc/models/`. The term surfaced in these docs and was kept deliberately: a
*lens* is what you get when you zoom in on one part of a system and write down,
rigorously, how it is defined and how it works — the design, not the code.

`doc/models/` is therefore a form of documentation, not implementation. It holds
the blueprints an architect produces before and alongside the source: the
declarations of *what* is being built and *how it must behave*, captured
precisely enough to reason about, but stopping short of the [[implementation]]
that lives under `src/`.

## A lens is a rigorous blueprint

Think of a lens as a [[blueprint]] in the strong, almost mathematical sense:

- It **zooms in** on one thing — a data structure, a domain rule, an algorithm,
  a protocol — rather than describing the whole system at once.
- It is **well defined**: types, relations, invariants, pre/postconditions, and
  step-by-step behavior are stated exactly, not sketched.
- It is **implementation-free**: it says how something is *defined* and *works*,
  not how one language or framework encodes it. The same lens admits many
  implementations.

"Blueprint" is arguably the plainer word; "lens" adds the two ideas that matter
here — a chosen *focus* and a chosen *magnification*. A model is a stack of
lenses, each trained on a different facet at the depth that facet needs.

## A lens is a format, not a folder

A lens is a *format* — a specification language of a particular kind, not a
special location. There is no `doc/models/lenses/` folder, and nothing is
required to be written in [[len]]. `doc/models/` is sorted by **facet** (data,
domain, behavior), and within each facet you pick whichever notation pins the
design down best:

- **[[len]] notation** — the formal option, when a facet has stabilized enough
  to state its types, relations, and contracts exactly.
- **Hyper-markdown (`.hmd`) prose with diagrams** — natural language mixed with
  diagrams that describe how the pieces link together. This is the
  [[behavior-driven-development]] / model-driven way to write a lens: a working,
  readable document rather than a formal grammar.

That prose-and-diagrams form is the **L0** lens — *working natural-language
documentation* — the loosest, most human end of the same spectrum [[len]]
formalizes. Even at L0, a lens borrows its vocabulary from the *len*
specification language: the same words for types, relations, contracts, and
obligations carry across, so an informal card and a formal `.len` file describe
the model in one shared language and can be tightened into each other later.

## What counts as a lens

Essentially every classical software-design artifact belongs here. The standing
subfolders sort them by facet:

- `doc/models/requirements/` — requirement blueprints: the constraints,
  acceptance criteria, and stakeholder intent that everything else is designed to
  satisfy. The most basic facet, referenced across the rest of the docs.
- `doc/models/data/` — data blueprints: database schemas, entity-relationship
  models, and [[data-modeling]] of the structures the system stores and moves.
- `doc/models/domain/` — domain blueprints: [[domain-driven-design]] models,
  bounded contexts, ontologies, and [[uml]] class/structure diagrams naming the
  concepts and their relations.
- `doc/models/behavior/` — behavioral blueprints: algorithms, state machines,
  sequence and activity diagrams, and executable-intent specs from
  [[test-driven-development]] and [[behavior-driven-development]].

The list is open-ended by design: anything an architect draws or specifies to
pin a system down — without writing its production code — is a candidate lens,
in whatever format suits it. See [[models]] for the facets as an index and how
they feed one another.

## Design, not implementation

The dividing line is grem's own: [[grem-cli]] stamps `doc/models/` as knowledge
and `src/` as code, and never blurs the two. A lens captures the *technical*
content — the algorithm, the contract, the shape of the data — at a level a
reader could implement from in any language. The moment a lens starts embedding
concrete production code, it has stopped being a lens.

## Writing lenses down formally

[[len]] is the experimental notation for taking a lens from L0 prose toward a
machine-checkable specification, through increasing **levels of formalism**
(L0 → L1 → L2). The rhyme is intentional: `len` is the notation, a *lens* is the
artifact it produces. Its point in this project is practical — lenses are how we
generate tests, so a tightened lens reads as [[test-driven-development]] and, at
the formal end, as a way to produce proofs. The dedicated [[len]] card covers
the language and its levels.

The [`doc/lenses`](../../../.grem/styles/doc/lenses/prompt.md) documentation
style turns a source doc — an issue, a wiki card, a rough note — into an L0/L1
lens under the right facet. See [[grem-cli]] for how grem places `doc/models/`.
